ep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa048cda6-76d1-4878-8d67-fbc8e38dbc93_1080x1350.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WZx7!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa048cda6-76d1-4878-8d67-fbc8e38dbc93_1080x1350.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WZx7!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa048cda6-76d1-4878-8d67-fbc8e38dbc93_1080x1350.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!WZx7!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa048cda6-76d1-4878-8d67-fbc8e38dbc93_1080x1350.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\" loading=\"lazy\"></picture><div class=\"image-link-expand\"><div class=\"pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset\"><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image buttonBase-GK1x3M\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 20 20\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke=\"var(--color-fg-primary)\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" class=\"icon-noB79L\"><g><path d=\"M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882\"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image buttonBase-GK1x3M\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2 icon-noB79L\"><polyline points=\"15 3 21 3 21 9\"></polyline><polyline points=\"9 21 3 21 3 15\"></polyline><line x1=\"21\" x2=\"14\" y1=\"3\" y2=\"10\"></line><line x1=\"3\" x2=\"10\" y1=\"21\" y2=\"14\"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p class=\"button-wrapper\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://vigeoretreats.com/retreat/the-wholehearted-woman-retreat/&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Learn More about this Retreat&quot;,&quot;action&quot;:null,&quot;class&quot;:&quot;button-wrapper&quot;}\" data-component-name=\"ButtonCreateButton\"><a class=\"button primary button-wrapper\" href=\"https://vigeoretreats.com/retreat/the-wholehearted-woman-retreat/\"><span>Learn More about this Retreat</span></a></p><div><hr></div><h3><strong>New to Retreats with Erin?</strong></h3><p>Here’s what to expect.</p><p class=\"button-wrapper\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://mindfulmoments.sg/retreats/what-to-expect&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Retreat Guide&quot;,&quot;action&quot;:null,&quot;class&quot;:&quot;button-wrapper&quot;}\" data-component-name=\"ButtonCreateButton\"><a class=\"button primary button-wrapper\" href=\"https://mindfulmoments.sg/retreats/what-to-expect\"><span>Retreat Guide</span></a></p>",
  "body_text": "Six sessions into our Dhammapada-inspired series, we arrive at the chapter called Flowers.\n\nAs a bee gathers nectar \nAnd moves on without harming \nThe flower, its color, or its fragrance, \nJust so should a sage walk through a village.\n\n(verse 49, trans. Gil Fronsdal)\n\nThe bee takes the nourishment it needs and leaves no wound behind. \n\nNow don’t be mistaken — this is not a call to have no impact at all. It is an invitation to thoughtful participation. Instead of asking what we can get from a place, we can be more cognisant of what we will leave behind once we have passed through it. \n\nCan I disagree with someone without humiliating them? Can I receive care from a community without taking it for granted?\n\nVerse 50 then turns our attention inward.\n\nIt is easy to look outward and begin sorting the world into what should and shouldn’t be done. This verse asks us to turn the other way first and ask what I have done, and what I have left undone. \n\nCultivating goodness is self-cultivation before it is anything else, and looking inward is what keeps it from hardening into self-righteousness.\n\nJust as from a heap of flowers \nMany garlands can be made, \nSo you, with your mortal life, \nShould do many skillful things.\n\n(verse 53, trans. Gil Fronsdal)\n\nGoodness is rarely one heroic act. Instead, it is often found in many ordinary moments: the tray returned at the food court, the trolley pushed back at the supermarket, the cruel post we decline to forward on our socials. \n\nA life is fragrant because of the little good things we repeatedly do.\n\nVerses 54 to 56 tell us that the scent of a virtuous person travels even against the wind. \n\nSo think of who has quietly influenced you on one way or another — an acquaintance who treats service staff with respect, a colleague who never joins the office gossip. \n\nFlowers are not fragrant because we are watching them. Likewise, goodness is not meant to be performative; it quietly influences through embodiment. \n\nVerses 58 and 59 leave us with the image of a sweet-smelling lotus growing in a heap of rubbish. \n\nWe have a saying — “no mud, no lotus.” The messiness of our lives is not what we have to clear away before practice begins. It is what we practice with, and through this mess, wisdom and compassion emerge, and the scent of goodness arrives. \n\nMay we move through the world gently, and thoughtfully. \n\n---\n\nAt Take A Pause Live on Insight Timer over 29-30 August 2026, we reflected on the kind of presence we’d like to leave in this world. We then shared a reading from Yung Pueblo’s The Way Forward. \n\nMeditation Practice\n\nTap Play to Practice Together:\n\nThis post is free for some time, and then archived for paid subscribers to support a sustainable, long-term sharing of mindful content. To support Erin’s work, upgrade to receive lifetime access to the full archive and ongoing updates.\n\nShare\n\n---\n\nThe Wholehearted Woman Retreat\n\n1–5 October 2026 · Siem Reap, Cambodia\n\nwith Erin Lee\n\n*Early bird rate available\n\nLearn More about this Retreat\n\n---\n\nNew to Retreats with Erin?\n\nHere’s what to expect.\n\nRetreat Guide",
